prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages job applications, interview feedback, and offers for recruiting.

The people involved are recruiters, hiring managers, interviewers, and coordinators. One person may hold recruiter and interviewer roles together, or recruiter and hiring manager roles together.

The resources are applications, interview feedback, and offers, with each application recording its candidate, referrer, hiring team, req code, PII sensitivity, and status, each interview feedback record recording its application, interviewer, candidate, and status, and each offer recording its application, extender, compensation, signing bonus, and status.

Each person making a request carries their PII clearance.

Each request carries whether it is from the corporate network, the current time, and whether executive approval is present.

A hiring manager may approve an offer only while the offer is drafted, unless the request falls within a permitted window.

When the request falls within a permitted window, the hiring manager may approve an offer that is not drafted.

A hiring manager must not approve an offer that is not drafted when the request is outside the permitted window.

A request by a hiring manager to approve an offer that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["recruiter", "hiringManager", "interviewer", "coordinator"];

entity Person {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    piiClearance: Long,
};

entity Candidate;

entity Application {
    candidate: Candidate,
    referrer?: Person,
    hiringTeam: Set<Person>,
    reqCode: String,
    piiSensitivity: Long,
    status: String,
};

entity InterviewFeedback {
    application: Application,
    interviewer: Person,
    candidate: Candidate,
    status: String,
};

entity Offer {
    application: Application,
    extender: Person,
    compensation: decimal,
    signingBonus: decimal,
    status: String,
};

action approveOffer appliesTo {
    principal: [Person],
    resource: [Offer],
    context: {
        isFromCorporateNetwork: Bool,
        currentTime: datetime,
        hasExecutiveApproval: Bool,
        isWithinPermittedWindow: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
